\section{The Same ISA, Two Call Contracts}

The \texttt{call} instruction pushes a return address and transfers control; it does not classify a type, preserve a temporary for the caller, or tell an unwinder how to recover saved registers. Those are ABI responsibilities. The \emph{System V AMD64 Architecture Processor Supplement}, edited by Matz, Hubi\v{c}ka, Jaeger, and Mitchell, specifies the System V side. Ed Jorgensen's \emph{x86-64 Assembly Language Programming with Ubuntu} demonstrates the use of that convention in assembly routines. Robert Dunne's \emph{Windows 64-bit Assembly Language Programming Quick Start} illustrates a different argument and stack layout on the same hardware. The comparison here is between the default user-space C conventions, not any operating system's system-call convention.

For ordinary scalar arguments, System V uses integer registers \texttt{rdi}, \texttt{rsi}, \texttt{rdx}, \texttt{rcx}, \texttt{r8}, and \texttt{r9}. It allocates scalar floating-point arguments from a separate sequence starting at \texttt{xmm0}. The Windows convention has four register argument \emph{positions}: a scalar integer uses the position's entry in \texttt{rcx}, \texttt{rdx}, \texttt{r8}, \texttt{r9}; a scalar floating-point argument normally uses the corresponding entry in \texttt{xmm0} through \texttt{xmm3}. The Windows caller also reserves 32 bytes of home space even when passing fewer than four arguments. The callee need not home the register arguments. In System V, the 128-byte red zone below the stack pointer can hold user-space temporaries not needed across calls. It is not the Windows home area and cannot be assumed to exist there.

\section{Worked Call: Interleaved Types}

Suppose a fixed-prototype function takes a 64-bit unsigned integer $a$, a double $b$, a 64-bit unsigned integer $c$, a double $d$, then a 64-bit unsigned integer $e$, and returns a 64-bit unsigned integer. The ordinary parameter assignments are:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
Parameter & System V & Windows \\
$a$ & \texttt{rdi} & \texttt{rcx} \\
$b$ & \texttt{xmm0} & \texttt{xmm1} \\
$c$ & \texttt{rsi} & \texttt{r8} \\
$d$ & \texttt{xmm1} & \texttt{xmm3} \\
$e$ & \texttt{rdx} & stack \\
Result & \texttt{rax} & \texttt{rax}
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
For a Windows caller at entry, with no pushes or other allocations yet, subtracting 40 from \texttt{rsp} reserves the four homes and the fifth argument slot and aligns the stack before \texttt{call}. Put $e$ at offset 32 from that adjusted pointer. The pushed return address moves the callee's view of $e$ to offset 40. The default 16-byte call-site alignment also applies in the corresponding System V scalar example, but neither the homes nor the fifth argument's stack slot is needed. A larger frame or stronger alignment requirement changes the adjustment; 40 is an outcome of this particular frame calculation, not a general prologue.

\section{Lower the Full Signature}

Do not classify aggregates by counting source-level arguments. In System V, small aggregates can be divided into eightbytes of different classes, and an attempted register assignment may roll back for the whole argument if registers run out. Windows passes many aggregates indirectly, so size and language-level layout rules can change both the caller's copies and the hidden arguments for a return value. The variadic rules also differ: System V uses \texttt{al} to communicate an upper bound on vector registers used, whereas Windows duplicates floating-point arguments in the corresponding general-purpose registers for variadic or unprototyped calls. A fixed signature's register map cannot stand in for these cases.

Lower argument shuffles as parallel assignments. For instance, a System V caller adapting four integer arguments to Windows needs \texttt{rdx} from \texttt{rsi} and \texttt{r8} from the incoming \texttt{rdx}. Writing the new \texttt{rdx} too soon corrupts the third argument. Respect the live sources when ordering copies, or use a temporary to break cycles. This issue comes after ABI classification: an accurate table of locations still does not tell the emitter how to get the values into them safely.

\section{Verify at the Call Boundary}

A thunk's preservation obligation comes from its incoming convention, even if its outgoing call uses a different preserved set. For example, a Windows-facing thunk calling a System V routine cannot expect that routine to preserve \texttt{rdi}, \texttt{rsi}, or the low 128 bits of \texttt{xmm6} through \texttt{xmm15}. Generate the enclosing platform's unwind information for the actual saves and stack adjustment. Test both call directions against independently compiled callees that return a checksum of distinct sentinels, clobber permitted volatile registers, and verify preserved registers and stack restoration. The dossier's \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/42-adaptive-abi-negotiation.md}{Adaptive ABI Negotiation} considers where such adapters fit in a negotiated interface.
